searchData={"items":[{"type":"task","doc":"Records an invitation without going through the dashboard.\n\nAn account is made only for someone who was invited, which leaves a fresh\ndatabase with nobody who can invite anyone — this is the way in. Invite\nyourself, sign in with that address, and the role is waiting; from then on the\ndashboard does this.\n\nIt is the same mechanism the dashboard uses, so the first admin arrives the\nway everyone else does, and the log says who let them in.\n\n    mix rb.invite someone@example.com admin\n    mix rb.invite someone@example.com          # contributor\n\nMail is attempted and a failure is reported, not fatal — the invitation stands\neither way, and can be sent again from the dashboard.","title":"mix rb.invite","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.Invite.html"},{"type":"task","doc":"Mix task to initialize the repo using a data.csv file in the project's root directory","title":"mix rb.load_data","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Mix.Task.run/1 .","title":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.run/1","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.html#run/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"RichardBurton keeps the contexts that define your domain\nand business logic.\n\nContexts are also responsible for managing your data, regardless\nif it comes from the database, an external API or others.","title":"RichardBurton","ref":"RichardBurton.html"},{"type":"behaviour","doc":"Authentication/authorization boundary. Delegates to the implementation\nconfigured under `:auth_service` (default `RichardBurton.Auth.Google`, swapped\nfor a Mox mock in tests).","title":"RichardBurton.Auth","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html"},{"type":"callback","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.authorize/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#c:authorize/2"},{"type":"callback","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.verify/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#c:verify/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns `:ok` if `subject_id` holds `role` or one that outranks it, `:error`\notherwise. Asking for the least privilege a route needs means a role added\nabove it is admitted without revisiting the route.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.authorize/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#authorize/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Authenticates an ID token, returning the identity the provider vouches for —\nthe subject and its verified email — or `:error`.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.verify/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Broadcasts that a person's access may have changed, on a PubSub topic for\nthat person.\n\n`topic/1` names the topic, and `changed/1` broadcasts `:access_changed` on\nit. The message does not say what changed. A subscriber reads the current\nstate from the database when it receives one, so the order of the messages\ndoes not matter, and an extra message costs only one more read.\n\nCall `changed/1` after the change is committed. A subscriber reads on its own\ndatabase connection, so it cannot see the change until the transaction that\nmakes it commits.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Broadcasts `:access_changed` on the topic of the person with `subject_id`.\nCall it after the change is committed.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.html#changed/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The PubSub topic for a person's access changes.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.topic/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.html#topic/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Validates the claims of an OIDC ID token: issuer, audience, subject, expiry\nand email. Signature verification is performed separately (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Google`); this module only checks the decoded claims.\n\nThe email comes from here rather than from whoever calls the login endpoint,\nand only when the provider vouches for it: a role is granted to an address, so\nan address the caller could choose would be a role the caller could choose. A\nprovider that will not say whether an address is verified is one this cannot\naccept a login from.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.identity/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html#t:identity/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns `{:ok, identity}` when every claim is valid, `:error` otherwise.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.validate/4","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html#validate/4"},{"type":"module","doc":"Double-submit CSRF token: a signed, session-bound token issued as a readable\n(`http_only: false`) cookie at login and echoed back in the `rb-csrf-token`\nheader on state-changing admin requests (see `RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf`).\n\nIt is signed with the endpoint's `secret_key_base`, so it can't be forged\nwithout the server secret, and it carries the session's subject so a token\nminted for one user can't authorize another's request. Defense in depth on top\nof the `SameSite=Lax` session cookie.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Name of the request header carrying the double-submit CSRF token.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.header_name/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#header_name/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.sign/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#sign/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.verify/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Google identity-provider implementation of the `RichardBurton.Auth` behaviour.\n\nID tokens are verified against Google's JWKS signing keys, which are cached\nand refreshed by `RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore` (using the\n`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google` provider) so that key rotation does not\nrequire a restart. The issuer, audience and expiry are all validated (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Claims`).","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google.html"},{"type":"behaviour","doc":"Behaviour for fetching a JSON Web Key Set (JWKS): the signing keys and issuer\nused to verify ID tokens, plus the cache lifetime the provider advertises.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.result/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html#t:result/0"},{"type":"callback","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.fetch/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html#c:fetch/0"},{"type":"module","doc":"`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS` implementation that fetches from Google: the issuer\nfrom Google's OpenID configuration and the signing keys from its OAuth2 certs\nendpoint (respecting the certs' `Cache-Control: max-age`).","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS` implementation that fetches nothing.\n\nUsed under test (and in any environment where no identity provider is\nconfigured) so the key store can run without making network calls. Any token\nverification against it fails cleanly.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Stub","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Stub.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Caches a provider's JWKS signing keys and keeps them fresh so that key\nrotation does not require restarting the server.\n\nProvider-agnostic: it caches whatever any `RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS`\nimplementation returns. The implementation is configured via the `:jwks_provider`\napplication env and can be overridden per instance with the `:provider` option.\n\nKeys are refreshed:\n\n  * on a schedule, honouring the `max-age` advertised by the provider; and\n  * lazily, whenever a token presents a `kid` that is not currently cached\n    (rate-limited by `:refresh_cooldown` to avoid hammering the\n    provider with bogus `kid`s).","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.child_spec/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns `{:ok, key}` for the cached key matching `kid`. On a cache miss, the\nkeys are refreshed once (rate-limited) before giving up with `:error`.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.fetch_key/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#fetch_key/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The issuer advertised by the provider, or nil if not fetched yet.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.issuer/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#issuer/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.start_link/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"behaviour","doc":"Behaviour for recaptcha authorization services","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html"},{"type":"callback","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.verify/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html#c:verify/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.verify/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Google Recaptcha implementation for RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Server-side (stateful) app sessions. Each session is a row in `auth_sessions`\nholding the authenticated user's `subject_id` and the SHA-256 hash of an opaque\ntoken. The raw token is set as the `rb-session` cookie and looked up on every\nrequest, so a session can be revoked instantly by deleting its row.\n\nLifetime is a **sliding idle timeout** (`:session_idle_timeout`) refreshed on\nuse, bounded by an **absolute cap** (`:session_max_age`) measured from creation.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.t/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns whether the session with `session_id` exists and has passed neither\nits idle timeout nor its absolute cap.\n\nUnlike `verify/1`, it does not slide the idle timeout, so checking a session\ndoes not extend it, and it does not delete an expired row.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.active?/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#active?/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Name of the httpOnly cookie carrying the raw session token.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.cookie_name/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#cookie_name/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Creates a session for `subject_id`, returning the raw opaque token to set as the cookie.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.create/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#create/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Sliding idle timeout in seconds (`:session_idle_timeout`).","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.idle_timeout/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#idle_timeout/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Absolute session cap in seconds — also the cookie max_age (`:session_max_age`).","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.max_age/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#max_age/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Revokes the session identified by `token` by deleting its row. Returns\n`{:ok, subject_id}` with the session's owner, or `:error` when no session has\nthat token.\n\nOn success it calls `RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1` for the owner. The\ndelete returns the `subject_id`, so the row does not have to be read first.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.revoke/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#revoke/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Revokes every session for `subject_id` (sign out everywhere), then calls\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1`. A caller that runs this inside a\ntransaction must call `Access.changed/1` again after the transaction commits.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.revoke_all/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#revoke_all/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Verifies a raw token, returning `{:ok, session}` with the session's row, or\n`:error`.\n\nRejects and deletes a session past its idle timeout or absolute cap, and\nslides the idle timeout forward on an active one.","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.verify/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for authors","title":"RichardBurton.Author","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Author.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Flatten authors to the names they are. A value that is not a list is returned\nunchanged.","title":"RichardBurton.Author.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Author.flatten([%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Clarice Lispector\"}])\n  [\"Machado de Assis\", \"Clarice Lispector\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Author.flatten(\"Machado de Assis\")\n  \"Machado de Assis\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Author.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Author.get_name/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#get_name/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Nest author names into the maps the schema casts.\n\nTakes the list the client and the flat schema both speak in. Splitting a\nwritten-down string into values is the CSV codec's, and is done before a value\nreaches here.","title":"RichardBurton.Author.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Author.nest([\"Machado de Assis\", \"Clarice Lispector\"])\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Clarice Lispector\"}]","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Author.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Author.search/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Author.search/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Utilities for serialization and deserialization","title":"RichardBurton.Codec","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Flattens a nested map, post-processing the resulting `{key, value}` pairs with the given `post` function.\n  Lists are not traversed.\n  Keys on nested maps are regenerated prepending the key of their parent map.\n\n  Returns the flattened map with string keys.","title":"RichardBurton.Codec.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{})\n      %{}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => 3}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: [1, 2, %{a: 1}]})\n      %{\"a\" => [1, 2, %{a: 1}]}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: 1, b: %{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3}})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b_a\" => 1, \"b_b\" => 2, \"b_c\" => 3}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: 1, b: [2, 3, %{a: 1, b: 2}], c: %{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3}})\n      %{ \"a\" => 1, \"b\" => [2, 3, %{a: 1, b: 2}], \"c_a\" => 1, \"c_b\"=> 2, \"c_c\"=> 3}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Codec.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Automatically nests a flat map.\n  Keys are grouped by their Longest Common Prefix (LCP) to define nesting points.\n  LCP is calculated considering atomic words separated by underscore:\n\n    `aBC` and `aDE do not have a common prefix\n    `a_BC` and `a_DE` have \"a\" as common prefix\n    `a_b_BC` and `a_b_DE` have \"a_b\" as common prefix\n\n  Returns the nested map with string keys.","title":"RichardBurton.Codec.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{})\n      %{}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => 3}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => 3}}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c_c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c_c\" => 3}}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c_c_c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c_c_c\" => 3}}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c_c: 3, b_c_d: 4})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => %{\"c\" => 3, \"d\" => 4}}}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Codec.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for countries","title":"RichardBurton.Country","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns every country, ordered by id, which is insertion order.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Every country the platform knows, by ISO alpha-2 code, as the table holds it.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.all_known/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#all_known/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"The codes of the countries a `country:` operator's value names.\n\nA value that is a complete name reaches that country alone. Otherwise it\nreaches every country whose name starts with it. So `country:US` is the United\nStates, not every country whose name begins \"us\".\n\nAn empty list is a filter no row satisfies, not the absence of one.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.answering/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#answering/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.answering(\"Reino Unido\")\n  [\"GB\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.answering(\"BRA\")\n  [\"BR\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.answering(\"zzzz\")\n  []","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.answering/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#answering/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"The ISO alpha-2 code a value names, or nil where it names no country.\n\nThe value has to be a complete name the country goes by: either ISO code, the\nname in either language, or one of the other names readers use.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.code_for/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#code_for/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.code_for(\"UK\")\n  \"GB\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.code_for(\"Estados Unidos\")\n  \"US\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.code_for(\"Braz\")\n  nil","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.code_for/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#code_for/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Flatten countries to the codes they are. A value that is not a list is\nreturned unchanged.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.flatten([%{\"code\" => \"BR\"}, %{\"code\" => \"US\"}])\n  [\"BR\", \"US\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.flatten(\"BR\")\n  \"BR\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Country.get_code/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#get_code/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Every country the platform knows, named in `locale`, ordered by that name.\n\nEach country is `%{id:, label:, article:, numeric:}`. `id` is the alpha-2 code\na publication stores, `label` its name in `locale` and `article` the article\nthat name takes. `numeric` is its ISO 3166-1 numeric code, as three digits,\nor nil for a country ISO assigns none. A locale the table has no names for is\nanswered in the default one.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.known/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#known/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The codes of the countries a term names outright.\n\nA name counts when its words appear consecutively in the term, with case,\naccents and punctuation folded away. So \"United States Kingdom\" names the\nUnited States and not the United Kingdom.\n\nA code counts when the term writes it in capitals, or when the term is that\ncode and nothing else. So \"machado DE\" asks for Germany and \"machado de assis\"\ndoes not.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.named_in/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#named_in/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.named_in(\"machado brazil\")\n  [\"BR\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.named_in(\"a study of translation\")\n  []","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.named_in/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#named_in/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Every name a country is searchable by: both its ISO codes, the name it is\nshown under in each language, and the other names a reader might type for it.\n\nDeduplicated, in no particular order — the search index folds them in, it does\nnot display them. A code the table does not hold answers with the code itself,\nso a country is always searchable by what is stored.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.names_for/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#names_for/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.names_for(\"NL\") |> Enum.take(4)\n  [\"NL\", \"NLD\", \"Netherlands\", \"Países Baixos\"]","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.names_for/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#names_for/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Nest country codes into the maps the schema casts.\n\nTakes the list the client and the flat schema both speak in. Splitting a\nwritten-down string into values is the CSV codec's, and is done before a value\nreaches here.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.nest([\"BR\", \"US\"])\n  [%{\"code\" => \"BR\"}, %{\"code\" => \"US\"}]","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"The codes of the countries a term reaches.\n\nA term that names countries outright reaches those, and `named_in/1` decides\nwhich. Otherwise the whole term is read as a name being typed, and every\ncountry whose name starts with it is reached: \"United\" reaches both the United\nStates and the United Kingdom.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.reached_by/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#reached_by/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.reached_by(\"machado brazil\")\n  [\"BR\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.reached_by(\"United States Kingdom\")\n  [\"US\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.reached_by(\"machado de assis\")\n  []","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.reached_by/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#reached_by/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Replaces each country in a changeset with its ISO code: \"UK\" becomes `GB`,\n\"Brasil\" becomes `BR`.\n\nA value naming no country, or more than one, is left as written for\n`validate_countries/1` to refuse by name.\n\nRuns before validation, so rows saying \"UK\" and \"GB\" store the same code,\nand the composite key counts them as one publication rather than two.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.resolve_countries/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#resolve_countries/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The countries `term` finds, named in `locale`.\n\nA country is found by either of its ISO codes, by the name it is shown under\nin any language, or by one of the other names readers type for it — so \"NL\",\n\"NLD\", \"Netherlands\", \"Países Baixos\" and \"Holanda\" all reach the same one.\n\nNames that begin with the term come before names that merely contain it, and\na name matched in full comes before both: one country's name can begin\nanother's, and typing a name in full should not put it behind the longer names\nit starts.","title":"RichardBurton.Country.search/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#search/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Country.validate_code/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_code/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Country.validate_countries/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_countries/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"An import document: a named batch of rows being prepared for the database,\nstored so that work on it can continue later.\n\nThe list of documents is shared. A document has no owner and no members,\nbecause everyone preparing the database works on the same data. Anyone who\nmay edit publications may open any document.\n\nA document's content is a Yjs document, and this module never parses it. A\nYjs document is a set of updates that can be applied in any order and more\nthan once, so storing one only needs appending updates and returning them.\nBecause the server does not parse Yjs, the deployment needs no precompiled\nnative dependency. Validating and submitting the rows use the publication\nendpoints, which take plain JSON.\n\nThese terms have specific meanings here:\n\n  * **update** — one change to the content, as Yjs encoded it, stored as\n    opaque bytes.\n  * **compaction** — one update that has the same effect as every update up\n    to a given id, written in their place. It keeps the number of updates a\n    long-lived document has to load small. Once written it is an ordinary\n    update, and nothing marks it or reads it differently.\n  * **archiving** — taking a document off the list without deleting its\n    content.\n  * **cursor** — the position a page of the list starts after: the\n    `updated_at` and id of the last document on the previous page.\n\n`rows` is the row count given with each write. The server cannot count rows\nin content it does not parse, so it stores the count as given and does not\ncheck it.","title":"RichardBurton.Document","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Appends an update to a document and stores `rows` as its row count.\n\nIt also sets the document's `updated_at` to now, which moves the document to\nthe top of the list. When `rows` is `nil`, the stored count is kept and the\nupdate is still appended, so a missing count never loses an update.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.append/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#append/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Archives a document by setting `archived_at`. The document leaves the list,\nand its updates are kept.\n\nArchiving does not delete anything, because the rows are a record of what was\nprepared. An archived document can still be found by id and can be\nunarchived.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.archive/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#archive/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Replaces every update of the document up to `through` with the single update\n`merged`.\n\nThe caller builds `merged`, because the server does not parse the content.\n`through` is the id of the last update the caller merged, as returned by\n`updates/2`. Updates with a larger id were appended after that read, so they\nare not in the merge and are kept.\n\nThe old updates are deleted and `merged` is inserted in one transaction, so a\nreader never gets both.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.compact/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#compact/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Creates a document from `attrs`, which must include a `name`.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.create/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#create/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns `{:ok, document}` for the id, or `{:error, :not_found}`.\n\nIt finds archived documents too, so an archived document stays reachable by\nid.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.find/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#find/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a page of documents, most recently changed first, as\n`%{entries: documents, more: boolean}`. `more` is true when more documents\nfollow the page.\n\nThe page is not scoped to a person, because the list is shared. It holds the\ndocuments that are not archived, or only the archived ones when\n`archived: true` is given. Documents with the same `updated_at` are ordered\nby id, newest first, so the order is the same on every read.\n\n`limit` sets the page size. It defaults to 50 and is capped at 200, so a\nread never returns the whole table.\n\n`after` is a cursor, and the page starts with the document that follows it.\nA cursor is used instead of an offset because a change moves a document to\nthe top of the list. With an offset, that move would shift every document\nbelow it by one, and a page could repeat or skip a document.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.page/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#page/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Renames a document.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.rename/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#rename/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Clears `archived_at`, which puts an archived document back on the list.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.unarchive/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#unarchive/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns `{updates, through}`: the document's updates in id order, and the id\nof the last one returned.\n\nApplying the updates in any order rebuilds the document's content. `through`\nis the id to pass to `compact/3` later, so that the compaction replaces only\nthe updates that were read and keeps any appended after them.\n\nWith `after: id`, only the updates with a larger id are returned. When there\nare none, `through` is the id that was given. No update is missed this way,\nbecause every write to a document's updates holds the document's row lock\nuntil it commits. Writes to one document therefore commit one at a time, in\nid order, and once an id has been read no update with a smaller id can still\nappear.\n\nEvery update after the given id is returned, compactions included. A\ncompaction deletes the updates it replaces, so any update left beside it is\none it does not include. Returning an update that a reader already has does\nno harm, because Yjs ignores an update it has already applied.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.updates/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#updates/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"One Yjs update to a document's content, stored as opaque bytes. Updates\nare written in the order of their ids.","title":"RichardBurton.Document.Update","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.Update.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for emails","title":"RichardBurton.Email","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"An email, as the contact form fills it in or as the system writes it.","title":"RichardBurton.Email.t/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Email.changeset/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Email.contact/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#contact/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Send mail as the system itself, rather than on someone's behalf.","title":"RichardBurton.Email.deliver/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#deliver/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for publications","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Validates `attrs` as `validate_all/2` validates each entry of a list.","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.validate/2","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#validate/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Validates each of `attrs_list` without writing anything, and returns the\nresults in order.\n\nA result is `{:error, errors}` when the attrs are invalid, `{:error,\n:conflict}` when a stored publication that is not deleted has their\ncomposite key, and `:ok` otherwise. The publication with the id\n`exclude_id` is left out of the key check, so an edit does not count against\nitself. The keys are looked up with one query for the whole list.\n\nThe lookup reads the publications table rather than the materialized view\nbehind this schema, so it also finds a publication inserted since the view\nwas last refreshed. Each entry is checked against the stored publications\nonly, not against the other entries. `Publication.Duplicates.resemblances/1`\nreports an entry that repeats an earlier one.","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.validate_all/2","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#validate_all/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The composite keys that stop an original book, a translated book or a\npublication from being stored twice. The database computes and enforces\nthem, and this module is how the application asks it.\n\nA *fingerprint* is a hash of a set of names. The keys are:\n\n  * an original book: its title, and the fingerprint of its authors' names;\n  * a translated book: the original book it translates, and the fingerprint\n    of its translators' names;\n  * a publication that is not deleted: its title, year and translated book,\n    and the fingerprints of its publishers' names and its country codes.\n\nThe database computes a fingerprint with `rb_set_fingerprint`, from the names\nsorted, so the same names in another order give the same fingerprint. Each\nstored fingerprint is kept up to date by triggers when a link is added or\nremoved, or a linked name changes (see the migration\n`ComputeFingerprintsInTheDatabase`). The database also has functions that\nfind the stored row with a key (see the migration\n`LookUpCompositeKeysInTheDatabase`), and `books_with_keys/1` and\n`publications_with_keys/2` call them for many keys at once.\n\nThe keys are checked when a transaction commits, because a row's links are\nwritten one by one after the row, and until the last one is written the row\ncan match another row's key. `settle/0` checks them earlier, so that a write\ncan report a conflict as its own result.","title":"RichardBurton.Identity","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"The fields of a flat publication that its composite key is built from. Names\nand country codes are lists, in any order. Other fields are allowed and\nignored.","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.key_fields/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#t:key_fields/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns, for each `{title, authors, translators}` in `keys`, the ids of the\nstored original book with that title and those authors, and of its stored\ntranslated book by those translators, as a pair. Either id is nil when there\nis no such book, and the translated book's is nil whenever the original\nbook's is. Names are lists in any order. The pairs come in the order of\n`keys`, from one query.","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.books_with_keys/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#books_with_keys/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the ids among `ids` of the publications that have the same key as\nanother publication that is not deleted, or whose translated or original\nbook has the same key as another book.\n\nOf two publications in `ids` with the same key, only the one with the higher\nid is returned. A publication outside `ids` counts whatever its id.","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.publications_in_conflict/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#publications_in_conflict/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns, for each of `publications`, the id of a stored publication that is\nnot deleted and has its key, or nil when there is none. The ids come in the\norder of `publications`, from one query. The publication with the id\n`excluded` is left out, so an edit does not match the publication it edits.\n\nEach of `publications` holds the fields in `t:key_fields/0`, where `:authors`\nare the translators.","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.publications_with_keys/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#publications_with_keys/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Checks the composite keys now, inside the current transaction, with one call\nto the database function `rb_settle` (see the migration\n`CheckCompositeKeysInOneCall`).\n\nReturns `:ok`, or `{:error, :conflict}` when a row has the same key as\nanother. After a conflict, only the check is rolled back, so the transaction\ncan still run queries, for instance to find the rows in conflict. The keys\nare deferred again afterwards, so the next write in the same transaction can\npass through states that match another key, as the first could.","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.settle/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#settle/0"},{"type":"module","doc":"A role held for an email address until whoever owns it signs in.\n\nAn admin cannot grant a role to someone who has never been here: the provider\nmints the subject id, and only on a first sign-in. So the grant waits on the\naddress instead, and the sign-in redeems it — the provider vouching for that\naddress *is* the confirmation, which is why no separate confirmation link is\nsent. Everything this rests on is therefore the identity provider's word: see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Claims`, which refuses a token that does not carry a\nverified email.\n\nSomeone who already has an account is given the role outright; there is\nnothing left to wait for.","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Admit a signed-in identity, if it is one this platform knows.\n\nAn account is made only for someone who was invited. Anyone may hold a Google\naccount, so a sign-in that created one regardless would mean the set of people\nwith accounts is decided by whoever tries — and revoking an account would mean\nnothing, since signing in again would make another. Being invited is what\nadmits you; the invitation is taken up in the same breath, so the role is\nalready in hand.\n\nSomeone who has been here before is admitted on the strength of their account.\nIf an invitation is also waiting for their address, they take it up, and\ntheir role is raised to the offered one when the offered one is higher. An\ninvitation never lowers a role.","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.admit/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#admit/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Every invitation, newest first — pending ones and the ones taken up.","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Withdraw a pending invitation.","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.cancel/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#cancel/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.get/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#get/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Offer `role` to an address, and say so by email.\n\nSomeone who has already signed in gets the role now — there is no sign-in left\nto wait for — and no invitation is recorded. The mail is sent after the grant\nlands, so a mail server having a bad day cannot cost someone their role; a\nfailure to send is reported so the dashboard can offer to send it again.","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.invite/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#invite/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Send a pending invitation's mail again.","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.resend/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#resend/1"},{"type":"behaviour","doc":"Behaviour for Mailer","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html"},{"type":"callback","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#c:send/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#send/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"STMP implementation for RichardBurton.Mailer behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Delivers an email.\n\nIf the email is delivered it returns an `{:ok, result}` tuple. If it fails,\nreturns an `{:error, error}` tuple.","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.deliver/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#deliver/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Delivers an email, raises on error.\n\nIf the email is delivered, it returns the result. If it fails, it raises\na `DeliveryError`.","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.deliver!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#deliver!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Delivers a list of emails.\n\nIt accepts a list of `%Swoosh.Email{}` as its first parameter.","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.deliver_many/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#deliver_many/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1 .","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.send/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#send/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"An original book: a title and the authors who wrote it.\n\nTwo original books with the same title and the same authors are the same\nbook, and the database keeps only one (see `RichardBurton.Identity`).\n`authors_fingerprint` is the fingerprint of the authors' names, which the\ndatabase writes, so the schema never writes it.","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Flatten a book to the shape a form speaks of it in: a title, and the authors\nit was written by.","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten(%RichardBurton.OriginalBook{\n  ...>   title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>   authors: [%RichardBurton.Author{name: \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n  ...> })\n  %{title: \"Dom Casmurro\", authors: [\"Machado de Assis\"]}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.preload/1","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#preload/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The original books a term names, by their title or by one of their authors.\n\nA book is entered as a unit — a title and who wrote it — and looking it up\nshould work from whichever half the person typing happens to know. Prefixes\nfirst, so what someone is part-way through typing wins; only when nothing\nstarts that way does the search fall back to similar spellings.","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/1","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/2","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"A publication: one translated edition of an original book, and the write paths\nthat change one.\n\nIdentity is a composite key: the title, the year, the translated book, and\nthe fingerprints of the countries and publishers. The database computes and\nenforces it (see `RichardBurton.Identity`), so the same edition cannot be\nstored twice. The near-duplicates that key cannot catch are\n`Publication.Duplicates`.\n\nFive words carry specific meanings here:\n\n  * **tombstone** — a soft-deleted row. `delete/2` stamps `deleted_at` rather\n    than removing anything, so the row, its sources and its history survive\n    and `restore/2` can return it. Every read path hides tombstoned rows, and\n    the composite-key index is partial so a tombstone never blocks a\n    re-import.\n  * **winner** and **losers** — in a merge, the record that survives and the\n    records collapsed into it. The winner keeps its identity; the losers\n    contribute their countries, publishers and sources, then are tombstoned.\n  * **absorbed** — the state of a record tombstoned by a merge rather than by\n    `delete/2`. It is not in the trash and `restore/2` refuses it with\n    `{:error, :absorbed}`; undoing the merge is what returns it.\n  * **compensate** — the action `undo/3` applies to reverse a recorded entry.\n    Nothing is erased: the compensating action is appended to the log as a new\n    entry, and is itself undoable.\n\nA publication's countries, publishers, translators and original authors are\nsets. The link tables keep no order, and a preload reads each set sorted, by\ncode for countries and by name for the others, as the search index lists\nthem. A history snapshot lists them in that order too, so two snapshots\ndiffer only when a set changed.\n\nEvery mutation records an entry in `Publication.History` inside the same\ntransaction, and signals `Publication.Index.Refresher` once per operation.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns every publication row, tombstones included, ordered by id, which is\nthe order they were inserted. Each row comes with its countries, publishers,\nsources and translated book preloaded.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"The publications someone deleted, most recently deleted first.\n\nA record absorbed by a merge is out of the database the same way, but nobody\ndeleted it and there is no putting it back: restoring one would recreate the\nduplicate the merge collapsed, with the survivor still holding what it took.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.all_deleted/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#all_deleted/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Soft-delete a publication: stamp `deleted_at`, so every read path hides it\nwhile its row, sources, and history survive — and `restore/3` can bring\nit back. The final state rides along in the history snapshot.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"One live publication, with its associations, or `nil`.\n\nA tombstone reads as missing here exactly as it does in the index and the\nsearch — a deleted record is gone from every read path, not merely hidden from\nthe list.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.find/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#find/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Inserts the publication `attrs` describes, and records it with a `created`\nhistory entry by `actor`, in one transaction.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publication}`, preloaded. Returns `{:error, errors}` when\n`attrs` is invalid, and `{:error, :conflict}` when a stored publication has\nthe same composite key. It is `Publication.Import.insert_all/2` with a batch\nof one.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.insert/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#insert/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Inserts the publications `attrs_list` describes in one transaction, all or\nnone, and records each with a `created` history entry by `actor`. See\n`Publication.Import.insert_all/2` for what it returns.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.insert_all/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#insert_all/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Collapse publications into one.\n\nThe winner keeps its identity and everything that names it — title, year, the\nwork it translates, who wrote and translated it. What the losers add is what\na record can hold more of: their countries and publishers join the winner's,\nand their sources are appended to its own. A source already recorded is not\nrecorded twice; elsewhere a publication may list the same line twice, but a\nmerge saying it twice is the merge showing, not the record meaning it.\n\nThe losers are then soft-deleted, and the whole act is recorded as one entry\non the winner naming what it took in — which is what lets it be undone as one\nact too, by `undo/3`.\n\nAnswers `{:error, :conflict}` when the merged record would collide with a\nthird publication, `{:error, :not_found}` when any of them is not here,\n`{:error, :self}` when a publication is asked to merge into itself, and\n`{:error, :no_losers}` when nothing was named to merge in.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.merge/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#merge/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.preload/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#preload/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Bring a soft-deleted publication back into the database. When `changes` are\ngiven, they are applied to the publication first, in the same transaction,\nand recorded as an update before the restore.\n\nOnly one that someone deleted. A record absorbed by a merge is out of the\ndatabase the same way, but it is not in the trash and does not come back on\nits own: `{:error, :absorbed}` says to take the merge apart instead.\n\nWhen a publication that is not deleted has the same composite key, which\nhappens when the same record was imported again or a name was corrected to\nmatch, the result is `{:error, {:conflict, twin}}`, where `twin` is that\npublication. Nothing is written then, not even the changes. Changes that do\nnot validate return their errors and write nothing either.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.restore/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#restore/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Undo one recorded change, by applying the action that compensates it.\n\nNothing is erased: the log is append-only, so the undo lands as a new entry of\nits own and is itself undoable. Eligibility is decided here rather than\ntrusted from the caller — the rule (see `History.undoable?/3`) is an invariant\nof the log, not an affordance of whichever client happens to be asking.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.undo/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#undo/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.update/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#update/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the publications with the ids `ids`, deleted ones included,\npreloaded, in the order of `ids`. Every id must be a stored publication's.\n\nThe countries, publishers, translated book, original book and both lists of\nauthors are read in one query that joins them, sorted the way the\nassociations' `preload_order` sorts them. The sources are read in a second\nquery, so that many sources do not multiply the rows of the first.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.with_ids/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#with_ids/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Serialization and deserialization utilities for publications","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Flatten a nested publication back to flat, string-keyed fields — the inverse of\n`nest/1`. Child lists become lists of the names or codes they hold, and the\n`translated_book` association is lifted back to top-level `authors` /\n`original_title` / `original_authors`. Accepts a `Publication` struct, a nested\nmap, a list, or a `%{publication:, errors:}` pair.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten(%{\n  ...>   \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>   \"translated_book\" => %{\n  ...>     \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}],\n  ...>     \"original_book\" => %{\n  ...>       \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>       \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n  ...>     }\n  ...>   }\n  ...> })\n  %{\"authors\" => [\"Helen Caldwell\"], \"original_authors\" => [\"Machado de Assis\"], \"original_title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\", \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\"}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Reads a CSV of publications: one row each, columns named by a header.\n\nThe header decides which column is which, so they may be written in any order\nand a column the file does not carry reads as empty. A name the table does not\nknow is ignored, which is what lets a spreadsheet keep a column of its own.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.from_csv/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#from_csv/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.from_csv!/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#from_csv!/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Nest a flat publication — a `FlatPublication` struct, a flat map, or a list of\nthem — into the shape the `Publication` changeset expects: multi-value fields\nbecome child maps, and the `translated_book`/`original_book` fields are\nre-parented under their association.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> nested =\n  ...>   RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest(%{\n  ...>     \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>     \"authors\" => [\"Helen Caldwell\"],\n  ...>     \"original_title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>     \"original_authors\" => [\"Machado de Assis\"]\n  ...>   })\n  iex> nested[\"translated_book\"][\"authors\"]\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}]\n  iex> nested[\"translated_book\"][\"original_book\"][\"title\"]\n  \"Dom Casmurro\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Writes publications as a CSV with a header, in the shape `from_csv/1` reads.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.to_csv/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#to_csv/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Finds publications that are likely the same record entered twice.\n\nThe composite key already blocks exact duplicates, so what remains are the\nnear-matches it cannot catch: a typo, a dropped accent, `St.` for `Saint`, a\ntranslator entered as \"R. Burton\" once and \"Richard Burton\" the next time.\n\nSix words carry specific meanings here:\n\n  * **candidate pair** — two publications similar enough to be worth review.\n  * **cluster** — a connected component of the candidate graph. If A matches B\n    and B matches C, all three form one cluster, because merging them is one\n    operation.\n  * **distinction** — a stored record that two publications are not the same,\n    so the review stops offering them. See `Distinction`.\n  * **ruled apart** — the state of a pair that has a distinction.\n  * **row** — a publication being imported that is not stored yet. It has no\n    id, so `resemblances/1` refers to it by its position in the list it is\n    given.\n  * **repeat** — a row with the same composite key as an earlier row in the\n    same list: the same title, year and original title, and the same names\n    in each list, in any order. The database would refuse to store both\n    rows, so a repeat is an error. A resemblance is not an error.\n\nSimilarity is trigram distance, the measure the author lookup also uses, over\nthe fields a duplicate would agree on. A pair is a candidate when the\ntranslators are similar, either the titles are or the original books are, and\nthe two agree on the year, the countries and the publishers.\n\nTwo publications agree on one of those fields when either of them has no value\nfor it. Otherwise they agree on the year when the years are equal, on the\ncountries when they share a country, and on the publishers when the\npublishers are similar or one side's contain the other's words, as \"Alfred A.\nKnopf\" contains \"Knopf\". So another edition of a book, with its own year,\ncountry or publisher, is not proposed. A record that leaves those fields\nempty can still be.\n\nThe translators are the required half because this is a database of\ntranslations: two people translating one book is the subject matter, not an\nerror. \"Dom Casmurro\" translated by Helen Caldwell and by John Gledson share a\ntitle and an original book and are two distinct publications.\n\nWhen one side leaves the year, countries or publishers out, nothing can tell\ntwo editions of one book from two records of one edition, so the rule proposes\nand a reviewer decides. Distinctions persist that decision, which is what\nmakes the queue converge.\n\n`clusters/0` and `resemblances/1` use the same similarity rule. `clusters/0`\ncompares stored records with each other. `resemblances/1` compares rows with\nthe stored records and with each other.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"The clusters worth reviewing, likeliest first.\n\nA cluster is a set of records joined by likeness: if A looks like B and B looks\nlike C, all three are one question, because merging them is one act. Pairs\nalready ruled apart are not edges, so ruling one out can split a cluster rather\nthan merely shrinking it.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.clusters/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#clusters/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Deletes the distinctions among these publications, returning the pair or\ncluster to the review queue.\n\nEvery pair among the ids is deleted, mirroring how `rule_apart/2` records\nthem: the decision covered the cluster, so reversing it does too.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.reconsider/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#reconsider/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns what each row resembles, among the stored records and the other rows\nin the list, and which earlier row it repeats.\n\nThe result has one entry for each row that resembles something or is a\nrepeat, in position order. An entry holds `position`, the row's index in\n`rows`; `stored`, the flat publications it resembles; `others`, the positions\nof the other rows it resembles; and `repeats`, the position of the first row\nwith the same composite key when the row is a repeat, or nil. A row that\nresembles nothing and is not a repeat has no entry.\n\nRows are string-keyed flat publications, the same shape validation takes.\nThis function writes nothing. It does not check or record distinctions,\nbecause a distinction links two stored records and a row is not stored.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.resemblances/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#resemblances/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Record that these publications are not the same record twice, so the reviewer\nis not asked about them again. Every pair among them is ruled apart: the answer\nis about the cluster the reviewer was shown.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.rule_apart/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#rule_apart/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The pairs someone has ruled apart, newest first, with the records themselves.\n\nA decision that cannot be seen cannot be taken back, and this is what a\nreviewer looks at to find one worth reconsidering.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.ruled_apart/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#ruled_apart/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"How alike two records must be to be worth asking about, between 0 and 1.\n\nRead at run time from `:duplicate_threshold`, so the sensitivity can be tuned\nagainst real data — too low floods the reviewer with false clusters, too high\nmisses real duplicates — without changing any code.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.threshold/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#threshold/0"},{"type":"module","doc":"One remembered \"these two are not the same\". Stored for the pair, lower id\nfirst, so the answer is the same however it is asked.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.Distinction","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.Distinction.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Append-only log of publication mutations: one immutable row per change, with\nthe action, a flattened snapshot of the record after it, the acting user, and\na per-publication version so each record's history reads as an ordered\nstream.\n\nImmutability is enforced by the database — a guard trigger rejects UPDATE and\nDELETE on the table (see the soft-delete migration) — and completeness by\nchokepoint: every mutation flows through the `Publication` context functions,\nwhich call `record/3` inside the mutating transaction, so a change and its\nhistory row commit or roll back together.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"The publications that are inside another record right now.\n\nA merged record's own history is still here, but nothing in it can be undone\nwhile it is absorbed — it is the merge that holds it, and the merge that gives\nit back. The answer is the whole log's, not one stream's: the entry that holds\na record sits on the winner, so asking a loser's own history would never find\nit.\n\nOnly the ids are read back: an entry's `absorbed` map carries a whole snapshot\nper record, and none of it is needed to answer which ones are held.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"The ids of the publications an entry took in, or gave back.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed_ids/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed_ids/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The publications an entry took in, or gave back, ordered by id.\n\nEach one carries its own id and none of the fingerprints: those are the\nserver's bookkeeping, not part of the record being shown.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed_records/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed_records/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Every recorded mutation across the database, newest first, each diffed.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Populate each entry's derived fields: what it changed relative to the previous\nversion of *its own* record, and whether it can still be undone.\n\nEntries come newest first, so a version's predecessor is the next element of\nits stream and the head is the first. Grouping by publication is what lets one\npass serve both the per-record log and the database-wide feed, where streams\ninterleave and the row above an entry usually belongs to a different\npublication. Pairing by position within the stream, rather than looking up\n`version - 1`, also holds if the version sequence ever has a gap.\n\nBoth answers need the whole stream, which is why they are computed here rather\nthan asked of one entry at a time.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.annotate/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#annotate/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"An update over its import — newest first, as the queries return them:\n\n    iex> alias RichardBurton.Publication.History\n    iex> [update, import] =\n    ...>   History.annotate([\n    ...>     %History{publication_id: 1, version: 2, action: \"updated\", snapshot: %{\"title\" => \"New\"}},\n    ...>     %History{publication_id: 1, version: 1, action: \"created\", snapshot: %{\"title\" => \"Old\"}}\n    ...>   ])\n    iex> update.diff.fields\n    %{\"title\" => %{from: \"Old\", to: \"New\"}}\n    iex> {update.undoable, import.diff, import.undoable}\n    {true, nil, false}\n\nThe head is always undoable; the import has nothing before it to diff against,\nand undoing it is not this entry's to offer once a later version exists.","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.History.annotate/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#annotate/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"The ordered history of one publication, newest first, each entry diffed.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.of/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#of/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Append one history row for a mutation, inside the caller's transaction.\n\nConcurrent mutations of the same publication serialize on its row lock, so\nthe max-version read is stable; the unique index on (publication_id, version)\nturns any residual race into a loud error instead of silent corruption.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record/4","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Appends a `created` history row for each of `publications`, with one insert,\ninside the caller's transaction. The publications must be ones the\ntransaction inserted, so each row is its publication's first version.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record_created/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record_created/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The state undoing an update produces: the record as it stands now, with\nexactly the fields that update changed put back to their pre-change values.\nFor a record's latest update that equals its previous snapshot; for an older\none, later changes to other fields survive.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.reverted_snapshot/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#reverted_snapshot/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"The flattened state stored with a history entry: the record as it stands,\nwith its associations collapsed to the flat fields the client speaks in.\n\nComparing two of these is how callers tell an edit that changed something\nfrom one that did not — the changeset cannot answer that, because sources\nare replaced wholesale on every save and so always look dirty.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.snapshot/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#snapshot/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Associations arrive flattened, as they are stored:\n\n    iex> alias RichardBurton.{Author, Country, OriginalBook, Publication, Publisher, TranslatedBook}\n    iex> publication = %Publication{\n    ...>   title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n    ...>   year: 1953,\n    ...>   countries: [%Country{code: \"US\"}],\n    ...>   publishers: [%Publisher{name: \"Noonday Press\"}],\n    ...>   sources: [],\n    ...>   translated_book: %TranslatedBook{\n    ...>     authors: [%Author{name: \"Helen Caldwell\"}],\n    ...>     original_book: %OriginalBook{\n    ...>       title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n    ...>       authors: [%Author{name: \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n    ...>     }\n    ...>   }\n    ...> }\n    iex> snapshot = Publication.History.snapshot(publication)\n    iex> {snapshot[:title], snapshot[:authors], snapshot[:countries]}\n    {\"Dom Casmurro\", [\"Helen Caldwell\"], [\"US\"]}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.History.snapshot/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#snapshot/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Actor attributed to mutations that happen outside a request.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.system_actor/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#system_actor/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Whether undoing this entry would yield \"as if it had never happened, with\neverything after it preserved\":\n\n  - a record's latest entry, always — the compensating action applies\n    directly: restore a delete, delete an import or a restore, revert an\n    update;\n  - an older *update*, only while the record is live and every field it\n    changed still holds the value it set — the revert then touches exactly\n    those fields and leaves later edits to others alone;\n  - never an older delete (a later restore already negated it) nor an older\n    import or restore (compensating those would discard the edits that\n    followed);\n  - a merge or an un-merge, only as the latest entry, by that same rule —\n    each is one act over several records, so compensating it means taking the\n    whole thing back: the record that survived gives up what it absorbed, and\n    the records that left come back. What followed an older one was written\n    against the merged record.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.undoable?/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#undoable?/3"},{"type":"module","doc":"Inserts a batch of new publications in one transaction, with a number of\nqueries that does not depend on the number of rows.\n\nThe batch is stored as its rows would be if each were imported alone, one\nafter another. A row links to the stored countries, publishers, authors and\nbooks it names, and to those an earlier row of the batch stored, and each\npublication is recorded in the history with a `created` entry. When a row is\ninvalid, or has the same composite key as a stored publication or an earlier\nrow, nothing is stored.\n\nA *row* is one entry of the batch, cast with `Publication.changeset/2`.\n\n`Publication.Links` finds or inserts the names and books that the rows use.\nThe publications, their links, their sources and their history entries are\nthen inserted with one statement per table. The composite keys are checked\nonce, after every row is written, with `Identity.settle/0`. A conflict leaves\nthe transaction usable, so the rows can still be read to find the first one\nin conflict.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Inserts the publications that `attrs_list` describes, and records `actor` as\nthe author of each one's `created` history entry.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publications}` in the order of `attrs_list`, each preloaded\nwith `Publication.preload/1`.\n\nReturns `{:error, {attrs, errors}}` for the first invalid row, where\n`errors` is what `Validation.get_errors/1` returns for it, before anything is\nwritten. When every row is valid, returns `{:error, {attrs, :conflict}}` for\nthe first row with the same composite key as a stored publication or an\nearlier row.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import.insert_all/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import.html#insert_all/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Full-text search over the publication index.\n\nA term is split on `:or` (or `:ou`) into **alternatives**, and a publication\nmatches if it satisfies any one of them.\n\nEach alternative contains free words and operators. Free words are matched one\nat a time and combined with AND, so every word has to match. An operator\nrestricts its value to a single field:\n\n    title:casmurro            search the title only\n    autor:machado             search the author, written in Portuguese\n    year:1950-1960            a range of years\n    -country:US               exclude\n    title:\"dom casmurro\"      these words, in this order\n\nAn alternative matches when both its words and its operators match.\n\nTerms used here, which also appear as tags in the code:\n\n  * **spelled out** — a term that uses quotes or a leading `-` but contains no\n    operator. Postgres already understands that syntax, so the term is handed\n    to `websearch_to_tsquery` as written instead of being parsed here. Tagged\n    `{:spelled_out, term}`.\n  * **criteria** — what a term becomes once it has been read: either\n    `{:spelled_out, term}` or `{:alternatives, alternatives}`. `matches/1` and\n    `ranking/1` are built from it, and it is the only thing that differs\n    between the two ways of reading a term.\n\nEvery word is read once, on its own: it matches as a prefix if the index holds\nwords beginning with it, and by resemblance if it does not. A word that has to\nfall back therefore does not drag the words beside it along with it, and the\nquery is built and run once rather than twice.\n\nBoth ways of reading a term match against a `tsvector` whose accents have been\nstripped, so the term has its accents stripped too before it is compared. See\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term` for the vocabulary a term is written in\nand the names each operator answers to.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The ids of every publication, in the order the index lists them: by title, then\nby id. The equivalent of `search_order/1` for a reader who is browsing rather\nthan searching.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all_order/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all_order/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.count/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#count/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Name of the response header carrying the index's total count.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.count_header/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#count_header/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"The full row for one id, or an empty list when there is none. Gives what\n`details/2` gives, for a single publication.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.detail/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#detail/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The full rows for the given ids, in that order — one page of an ordering that\n`search_order/1` or `all_order/0` returned earlier.\n\nPass `nil` as the term for a plain listing. Pass the term itself and each row\ncomes back with an excerpt of every field that matched it.\n\nAn id that is no longer in the database is left out. That is what a deletion\nlooks like after the order was fixed: a gap in the page, rather than rows\nshifting up or repeating.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.details/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#details/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a query for the publications a term matches, or for every publication\nwhen the term is `nil`. The query has no ordering.\n\nIt always returns a query. When the term has nothing to search for, the query\nreturns no rows, so callers can count over it without a separate check.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.matching/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#matching/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"How many publications a page holds.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.per_page/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#per_page/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The publications a term matches.\n\nThe term is split on `:or` (or `:ou`) into alternatives, and a publication\nmatches if it satisfies any one of them. Within an alternative, words are\ncombined with AND, so every word has to match. That is why searching a full\ntitle returns only that title, while \"one title or another\" returns both.\n\nA single word only has to match one of the indexed words it could be, so a\nhalf-typed word matches anything it might still become. The term is tried as\ntyped first; only if that matches nothing is it retried fuzzily, and a word\nthat matches nothing is then dropped rather than emptying the whole result.\n\nThis returns everything at once, with no paging, for callers that need the\nwhole result — the CSV export downloads the database rather than a page of it.\nA reader goes through `search_order/1` and `details/2` instead.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The ids of every publication a term matches, in the order they should be read,\nor `:none` if the term matches nothing at all.\n\nThe order is decided once, here. The reader then pages through those ids with\n`details/2` and sees a stable list, because the order was fixed at the moment\nthe search ran. Without that, rows could shift, be skipped or appear twice as\nthe database changed between one page and the next.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search_order/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search_order/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Publications that have no sources recorded, ordered by id. This is the queue\nthe sources backfill works through, and the order has to be stable so it can be\nresumed.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.without_sources/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#without_sources/0"},{"type":"module","doc":"Builds the highlighted text that shows why a publication matched a search.\n\nA search result tells you which publications matched, but not what in them\nmatched. This asks Postgres for that: for each field, the part of its text\nthat the search matched, with the matched words wrapped in `[[ ]]`. The result\ngoes in the row's virtual `excerpts` map, keyed by field, and is `nil` for any\nfield the search did not match.\n\nThe highlighting is produced by the same query that decides the match, because\nonly the database holds what is needed to place it. It knows which spellings\nthe search configuration treats as the same word and where one word ends, and\nit knows which field each part of the term applied to. `title:night` has to\nhighlight the title and nothing else, and a list of matched words that does\nnot record which field each was searched in cannot express that.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **excerpt** — the matching text of one field. Short fields come back in\n    full. `sources` is an array, which the search treats as one long\n    string, so it comes back as a short window around the match instead.\n  * **widening** — one word that the search matched with something other than\n    what was typed, either because the word is a prefix of several indexed\n    words or because it matched none and fell back to ones resembling it. A\n    word matched exactly is not a widening, and is not reported. See\n    `resolution/1`.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"One word the search matched with something other than what was typed: the word\nas typed, the indexed words it matched, and the field it was searched in. The\nfield is nil for a free word, which is searched in every field.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.widening/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#t:widening/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Lists the words the search matched with something other than what was typed.\n\nEach entry gives the word as it was typed, the indexed words it actually\nmatched, and the field it was searched in. The field is `nil` for a free word,\nwhich is searched in every field. Entries come back in the order the words were\ntyped.\n\nWords the search matched exactly are left out, since reporting that `machado`\nmatched `machado` adds nothing. What is worth reporting is that `Maries`\nmatched `marias`, `marie` and `mario`, because those results correspond to no\nword in the term as it was written.\n\nThis uses the same word resolution the excerpts use, so the words reported here\nare exactly the words highlighted in the rows. Two cases return nothing: a\nquoted value, which is matched exactly and so is never widened, and a term\nPostgres parses itself (see `RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query`).","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.resolution/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#resolution/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Adds the `excerpts` and `marked` maps to a query.\n\n`excerpts` answers per field: the matching text of the whole field, or nil\nwhere the search did not match it. `marked` answers per value: a list lined up\nwith the field's own, holding each value's matching text, or nil where that\nvalue did not match.\n\nBoth come from the term alone, read the way the search read it. A field no part\nof the term searched gets no tsquery, and so neither an excerpt nor marks.\n\n`marked.countries` holds the matched country's code rather than marked text,\nsince naming a country needs a locale the index does not have.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.select/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#select/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Maps the words someone typed onto the words the index actually contains.\n\n`search_keywords` is a materialized view holding every distinct word in the\nsearch documents. Terms are matched against it one word at a time, because\nneither kind of match works on anything longer: a prefix is the beginning of a\nsingle word, and trigram similarity between a word and a whole phrase drops\ntowards zero as the phrase gets longer.\n\nThe caller picks one of two modes:\n\n  * `:prefix` — the indexed words that start with the given word. Used when\n    matching a term as typed.\n  * `:fuzzy` — the indexed words that merely resemble it. Used for the second\n    attempt, after nothing matched as typed.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"The indexed words a single word stands for, in the given mode.\n\nThe index holds words with their accents folded away, so the word is folded\nthe same way before it is compared to them.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.resolve/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#resolve/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The indexed words a given word matches, and how it matched them: the words it\nis a prefix of, or the words it resembles if it is a prefix of none.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.standing_for/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#standing_for/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Splits a term into the individual words it is made of.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#words/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words(\"dom casmurro\")\n    [\"dom\", \"casmurro\"]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words(\"  dom   casmurro  \")\n    [\"dom\", \"casmurro\"]","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#words/1-examples"},{"type":"module","doc":"Builds the query a parsed search term resolves to.\n\n`Publication.Index.Term` says what a reader asked for; this says how to ask it\nof the database. Nothing here reads a publication. It returns the `WHERE` and\n`ORDER BY` fragments `Publication.Index` runs.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **criteria** — a term ready to query with, and a tag the code matches on:\n    `{:spelled_out, term}` for one handed to Postgres verbatim, or\n    `{:alternatives, alternatives}` for one parsed into words and filters.\n  * **predicate** — one condition a publication has to satisfy, held as an Ecto\n    `dynamic` so it can be combined with others and spliced into the `WHERE`.\n\nA free word is matched against the whole search document; an operator against\nthe single column it names. That is why a term carrying operators cannot be\none tsquery string, and is composed from predicates instead.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"A parsed term as criteria: for each alternative, the tsquery for its free words\nand the filters that narrow it.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.criteria/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#criteria/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Whether criteria found anything to search for. A term whose every word resolved to\nnothing, and which carries no filter, asks for nothing at all.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.empty?/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#empty?/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Writes a word as a tsquery lexeme, in quotes, so that any punctuation in it is\nread as part of the word instead of as tsquery syntax.\n\nInside those quotes a backslash escapes whatever follows it, so backslashes and\nquotes both have to be escaped themselves. A word ending in an unescaped\nbackslash would otherwise escape the closing quote, and Postgres would reject\nthe whole tsquery.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#lexeme/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme(\"caldwell\")\n    \"'caldwell'\"\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme(\"o'brien\")\n    \"'o''brien'\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#lexeme/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"The `WHERE` clause for the given criteria.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.matches/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#matches/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The `ORDER BY` expression for the given criteria: how well each row matches.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.ranking/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#ranking/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Whether a term quotes a phrase or negates a word, which Postgres reads itself\nthrough `websearch_to_tsquery`.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.spelled_out?/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#spelled_out?/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"A word as the tsquery that matches it.\n\nReads the word through `Keywords.standing_for/1` and renders whichever way it\nmatched: a word that begins indexed words becomes a prefix query, and one that\nbegins none becomes the list of words it resembles. A word the index does not\nhold at all has no tsquery, and the caller decides what that means.\n\nThis is the one place a word becomes a query, so a row is highlighted by the\nsame thing that matched it.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.word_query/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#word_query/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Owns the read model: write paths call `refresh/0` once per logical operation\n(a bulk insert, an update), and the refresher rebuilds the `flat_publications`,\n`search_documents` and `search_keywords` materialized views — the flattened\nrows the index lists, and the search documents and keywords stacked on them.\n\nThe strategy comes from `config :richard_burton, :search_index_refresh`:\n\n  * `{:debounced, ms}` (default — dev and prod): signals coalesce; the views\n    rebuild once after a quiet window, outside any transaction, with\n    `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` so searches never block and\n    writes never wait. Search lags a write by up to the window — the\n    documented trade-off.\n  * `:sync_concurrent` (e2e): rebuild inline before `refresh/0` returns —\n    deterministic for browser tests while exercising the same CONCURRENTLY\n    path production runs.\n  * `:sync` (test): rebuild inline with a plain `REFRESH` — the ExUnit\n    sandbox wraps tests in transactions, where CONCURRENTLY is not allowed.\n\nIn debounced mode the refresher also rebuilds once on boot, covering signals\nlost to a restart and data seeded while the app was down.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.child_spec/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Signal that the index is stale. Returns after the rebuild in sync modes.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.refresh/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#refresh/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.start_link/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for searchable documents","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchDocument","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchDocument.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for searchable documents","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchKeyword","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchKeyword.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Parses a search term into the structure `Publication.Index` builds a query from.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **term** — everything the reader typed, as a single string.\n  * **alternative** — a part of a term that can satisfy it on its own. `:or`\n    (or `:ou`) separates them, so a term without `:or` is one alternative.\n  * **operator** — the `field:value` form as written, such as `title:casmurro`.\n  * **filter** — an operator after parsing: the column it names, its value, and\n    whether it was quoted or negated. The operator is what gets typed; the\n    filter is what the query gets built from.\n\nAn alternative holds free words and filters, and matches only when both do.\n\n    title:casmurro          matches the title only\n    year:1950-1960          matches a year range\n    -country:US             excludes\n    title:\"dom casmurro\"    matches the words in that order\n    title:(dom casmurro)    matches both words in any order\n\nAn operator's value is matched the same way a free word is — by prefix, falling\nback to similar words — unless it is quoted, in which case it is matched\nexactly as written.\n\nEvery operator answers to an English name and a Portuguese one. Case and accents\nare ignored when matching them, and a two-word name may be written with either a\nhyphen or an underscore.\n\nAnything before a colon that is not a known name is not an operator at all:\n`foo:bar` is parsed as ordinary text. That way a colon typed inside a title does\nnot break the search.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.alternative/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#t:alternative/0"},{"type":"type","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.filter/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#t:filter/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"A word in the form the index holds it: lowercased, with accents removed.\n\nOperator names are compared in this form, so `TÍTULO`, `título` and `titulo` are\nall the same name and it only has to be listed once. The search configuration's\n`unaccent` puts values into the same form, which makes this the way to tell\nwhether a word the index returned is the one that was typed.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.fold/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#fold/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.fold(\"Angústia\")\n    \"angustia\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.fold/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#fold/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"The name used to write an operator on this field.\n\nA field answers to several names. This returns the English one, which is the\nname reported for the field and the name that can be written back into a term.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#name/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name(:original_authors)\n    \"author\"\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name(:sources)\n    \"source\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#name/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Parses a term into its alternatives, each holding the free words and the field\nfilters it contains. A term with no operators parses to one alternative of\nwords.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#parse/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"dom casmurro\")\n    [%{words: [\"dom\", \"casmurro\"], filters: []}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"casmurro :or iracema\")\n    [%{words: [\"casmurro\"], filters: []}, %{words: [\"iracema\"], filters: []}]\n\nAn operator becomes a filter naming the column it scopes to, here through its\nPortuguese name:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"ano:1950\")\n    [%{words: [], filters: [%{field: :year, value: \"1950\", exact: false, negated: false}]}]\n\nA leading minus negates it, and words alongside it stay free:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"machado -country:US\")\n    [\n      %{\n        words: [\"machado\"],\n        filters: [%{field: :countries, value: \"US\", exact: false, negated: true}]\n      }\n    ]\n\nA quoted value is exact; a bracketed one is not:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(~s(title:\"dom casmurro\"))\n    [\n      %{\n        words: [],\n        filters: [%{field: :title, value: \"dom casmurro\", exact: true, negated: false}]\n      }\n    ]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"title:(dom casmurro)\")\n    [\n      %{\n        words: [],\n        filters: [%{field: :title, value: \"dom casmurro\", exact: false, negated: false}]\n      }\n    ]\n\nA prefix that names no field is read as free text rather than as an operator:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"foo:bar\")\n    [%{words: [\"foo:bar\"], filters: []}]","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#parse/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Whether a term contains no operators, and so searches no field in particular.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#plain?/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?(RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"dom casmurro\"))\n    true\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?(RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"title:casmurro\"))\n    false","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#plain?/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Parses a `year` operator value into `{from, to}`, where either bound may be\nnil: `1950` gives `{1950, 1950}`, `1950-1960` gives `{1950, 1960}`, `1950-`\ngives `{1950, nil}` and `-1960` gives `{nil, 1960}`. Returns `:none` for\nanything else, which the caller matches nothing against rather than dropping\nthe operator.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#span/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"1950\")\n    {1950, 1950}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"1950-1960\")\n    {1950, 1960}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"1950-\")\n    {1950, nil}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"-1960\")\n    {nil, 1960}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"recently\")\n    :none\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"-\")\n    :none","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#span/1-examples"},{"type":"module","doc":"Counts the publications in the index, and the distinct works and names in\nthem. `describe/1` counts either every publication or the ones a search\nmatches.\n\nThe counts are read from `flat_publications`, the same rows the index lists,\nso they agree with the index. A write is included in both only after\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher` refreshes the index.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **work** — an original book, identified by its original title and\n    original authors.\n  * **translation** — one translated text of a work, identified by the work\n    and its translators. Each publication belongs to one translation. A\n    translation has several publications when the same text is published\n    again, in another year, by another publisher or in another country.\n  * **retranslated** — describes a work that has more than one translation.\n  * **first translation**, **retranslation** and **reissue** — the three\n    kinds of publication. A publication is a reissue when an earlier\n    publication of its translation exists. Otherwise it is the first\n    publication of its translation, which is a first translation when its\n    translation is the work's earliest, and a retranslation when the work had\n    an earlier translation. Publications are ordered by year, and by id\n    within a year, and this order is taken over the whole index, so a search\n    does not turn a reissue into a first translation.\n  * **debut** — the year of an original author's first publication in the\n    index.\n  * **leading** — the names in one field that appear in the most\n    publications, at most ten. Names with the same count are sorted\n    alphabetically.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a map of counts for the publications a term matches, or for every\npublication when the term is `nil`.\n\nThe map has these keys:\n\n  * `publications` — the number of publications.\n  * `years` — the first and the last year of publication, or `nil` when there\n    are no publications.\n  * `annual` — the number of publications in each year, from the first year\n    to the last, split by country. `countries` holds the codes of the two\n    countries with the most publications, or fewer when there are fewer.\n    Each entry of `years` holds `counts`, the publications in each of those\n    countries in the same order, and `elsewhere`, the publications in no\n    country of `countries`. A publication that names several of them counts\n    under the first one in `countries`.\n  * `decades` — the number of publications in each decade, from the first\n    decade to the last, split into `first_translations`, `retranslations` and\n    `reissues`. Decades with no publications are included.\n  * `debuts` — the number of original authors whose debut falls in each\n    decade, counting the authors the publications name. The decades run\n    from the first debut to the last, including decades with none.\n  * `totals` — the number of distinct works, original authors, translators,\n    publishers and countries in the publications.\n  * `original_authors`, `translators`, `publishers` — the leading names in\n    each field, each with its number of publications. Each translator also\n    has `years`, the number of publications in each year that has any,\n    earliest first.\n  * `pairs` — up to ten pairs of an original author and a translator, those\n    that appear together in the most publications, each with that number.\n    Pairs with the same count are sorted by author and then translator.\n  * `countries` — every country of publication by its code, each with its\n    number of publications, highest first.\n  * `retranslated` — up to ten retranslated works, those with the most\n    translations, each with its number of translations and `timeline`: the\n    year of each translation's first publication in the index, with its\n    translators, earliest first.\n  * `sourced` — the number of publications that cite at least one source.\n\nA search is resolved to the publications it matches once, and the counts are\nread from those publications. When it matches none, no other query runs.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.describe/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.html#describe/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Finds the stored rows that publications link to, and inserts the ones that\nare missing: their countries, their publishers, and their translated books,\nwith the original books and authors those link to. It works on a list of\npublications at once, with a number of queries that does not depend on the\nlength of the list.\n\nAn *entry* is a publication as `Ecto.Changeset.apply_changes/1` returns it\nfrom a valid `Publication.changeset/2`. It holds names, but no ids.\n\nAn original book is identified by its title and its authors' names, and a\ntranslated book by its original book and its translators' names, as\n`RichardBurton.Identity` describes. An entry's *book key* holds its original\ntitle, its original authors' names and its translators' names, with the\nnames sorted. So the same names in another order give the same key, and\nentries with the same key share one original book and one translated book.\n\nAnother transaction can insert the same name at the same time. The insert\nhere then waits for that transaction to finish, skips the name once it has\ncommitted, and reads the row it stored instead.\n\nBooks have no unique index to wait on, because their keys are checked when\nthe transaction ends. So before it looks the books up, `resolve/1` takes an\nadvisory lock on each original book's key, held until the transaction ends.\nAnother transaction that names one of those original books waits for the\nlock, and then finds the books this one stored. Every translated book has an\noriginal book, so the same lock covers both.\n\nNames, and then the locks, are taken in sorted order, and every name is\ninserted before any lock is taken. So two transactions that need some of the\nsame names or books wait for each other in the same order, and neither waits\nfor the other while the other waits for it.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.html"},{"type":"type","doc":"The stored rows one entry links to: the id of its translated book, and its\ncountries and publishers, loaded, in the order the entry lists them.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.t/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the stored rows each of `entries` links to, in the order of\n`entries`, and inserts the ones that are missing, inside the caller's\ntransaction.","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.resolve/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.html#resolve/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for publishers","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Flatten publishers to the names they are. A value that is not a list is\nreturned unchanged.","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten([%{\"name\" => \"Noonday Press\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Penguin\"}])\n  [\"Noonday Press\", \"Penguin\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten(\"Noonday Press\")\n  \"Noonday Press\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.get_name/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#get_name/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Nest publisher names into the maps the schema casts.\n\nTakes the list the client and the flat schema both speak in. Splitting a\nwritten-down string into values is the CSV codec's, and is done before a value\nreaches here.","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publisher.nest([\"Noonday Press\", \"Penguin\"])\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Noonday Press\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Penguin\"}]","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publisher.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.search/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.search/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Used for executing DB release tasks when run in production without Mix\ninstalled.","title":"RichardBurton.Release","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Release.migrate/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html#migrate/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Release.rollback/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html#rollback/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The Ecto repository, extended with `insert_in_chunks/3`, which inserts a\nlist longer than one statement can hold.","title":"RichardBurton.Repo","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.aggregate/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.aggregate/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#aggregate/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.aggregate/4 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.aggregate/4","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#aggregate/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.all/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.all/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#all/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.all_by/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.all_by/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#all_by/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.checked_out?/0 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.checked_out?/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#checked_out?/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.checkout/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.checkout/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#checkout/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.child_spec/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.config/0 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.config/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#config/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.default_options/1 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.default_options/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#default_options/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete!/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete_all/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete_all/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete_all/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Forces all connections in the repo pool to disconnect within the given interval.\n\nOnce this function is called, the pool will disconnect all of its connections\nas they are checked in or as they are pinged. Checked in and idle connections\nwill be randomly disconnected within the given time interval.\n\nIf the connection has a backoff configured (which is the case by default),\ndisconnecting means an attempt at a new connection will be done immediately\nafter, without starting a new process for each connection. However, if backoff\nhas been disabled, the connection process will terminate. In such cases,\ndisconnecting all connections may cause the pool supervisor to restart\ndepending on the max_restarts/max_seconds configuration of the pool,\nso you will want to set those carefully.\n\nIf you need connections to be restarted periodically, set the `:max_lifetime`\noption in your repository configuration instead.","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.disconnect_all/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#disconnect_all/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.exists?/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.exists?/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#exists?/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Executes an EXPLAIN statement or similar for the given query according to its kind and the\nadapter in the given repository.","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"# Postgres\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, Post)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\"\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.explain(Repo, :all, Post)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\"\n\n    # MySQL\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, from(p in Post, where: p.title == \"title\")) |> IO.puts()\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n    | id | select_type | table | partitions | type | possible_keys | key  | key_len | ref  | rows | filtered | Extra       |\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n    |  1 | SIMPLE      | p0    | NULL       | ALL  | NULL          | NULL | NULL    | NULL |    1 |    100.0 | Using where |\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n\n    # Shared opts\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, Post, analyze: true, timeout: 20_000)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=443) (actual time=0.013..0.013 rows=0 loops=1)\\nPlanning Time: 0.031 ms\\nExecution Time: 0.021 ms\"\n\nIt's safe to execute it for updates and deletes, no data change will be committed:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(Repo, :update_all, from(p in Post, update: [set: [title: \"new title\"]]))\n    \"Update on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=449)\\n  ->  Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=449)\"\n\nThis function is also available under the repository with name `explain`:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, from(p in Post, where: p.title == \"title\"))\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\\n  Filter: ((title)::text = 'title'::text)\"","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Built-in adapters support passing `opts` to the EXPLAIN statement according to the following:\n\nAdapter          | Supported opts\n---------------- | --------------\nPostgrex         | `analyze`, `verbose`, `costs`, `settings`, `buffers`, `timing`, `summary`, `format`, `plan`, `wrap_in_transaction`\nMyXQL            | `format`, `wrap_in_transaction`\n\nAll options except `format` are boolean valued and default to `false`.\n\nThe allowed `format` values are `:map`, `:yaml`, and `:text`:\n  * `:map` is the deserialized JSON encoding.\n  * `:yaml` and `:text` return the result as a string.\n\nThe built-in adapters support the following formats:\n  * Postgrex: `:map`, `:yaml` and `:text`\n  * MyXQL: `:map` and `:text`\n\nThe `wrap_in_transaction` option is a boolean that controls whether the command is run inside of a\ntransaction that is rolled back. This is useful when, for example, you'd like to use `analyze: true`\non an update or delete query without modifying data. Defaults to `true`.\n\nThe `:plan` option in Postgrex can take the values `:custom` or `:fallback_generic`. When `:custom`\nis specified, the explain plan generated will consider the specific values of the query parameters\nthat are supplied. When using `:fallback_generic`, the specific values of the query parameters will\nbe ignored. `:fallback_generic` does not use PostgreSQL's built-in support for a generic explain\nplan (available as of PostgreSQL 16), but instead uses a special implementation that works for PostgreSQL\nversions 12 and above. Defaults to `:custom`.\n\nAny other value passed to `opts` will be forwarded to the underlying adapter query function, including\nshared Repo options such as `:timeout`. Non built-in adapters may have specific behaviour and you should\nconsult their documentation for more details.\n\nFor version compatibility, please check your database's documentation:\n\n  * _Postgrex_: [PostgreSQL doc](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-explain.html).\n  * _MyXQL_: [MySQL doc](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/explain.html).","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3-options"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get!/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get!/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get!/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_by/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_by/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_by/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_by!/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_by!/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_by!/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_dynamic_repo/0 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_dynamic_repo/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_dynamic_repo/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.in_transaction?/0 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.in_transaction?/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#in_transaction?/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert!/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_all/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_all/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_all/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Inserts `entries` with `insert_all/3`, as many per statement as fit within\nPostgres's limit of 65,535 parameters, and returns the rows the statements\nreturned, in the order of `entries`. It returns an empty list when `opts`\nhas no `:returning`, and sends no statement when `entries` is empty.\n\nWhen `schema_or_source` is a schema, each entry gets the values the schema\ngenerates on insert, such as its timestamps, as `insert/2` gives them. Every\nentry must have the same keys.","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_in_chunks/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_in_chunks/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_or_update/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_or_update/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_or_update/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_or_update!/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_or_update!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_or_update!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.load/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.load/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#load/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.one/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.one/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#one/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.one!/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.one!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#one!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.preload/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.preload/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#preload/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.prepare_query/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.prepare_query/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#prepare_query/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.prepare_transaction/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.prepare_transaction/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#prepare_transaction/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.put_dynamic_repo/1 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.put_dynamic_repo/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#put_dynamic_repo/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Runs a custom SQL query.\n\nIf the query was successful, it will return an `:ok` tuple containing\na map with at least two keys:\n\n  * `:num_rows` - the number of rows affected\n  * `:rows` - the result set as a list. `nil` may be returned\n    instead of the list if the command does not yield any row\n    as result (but still yields the number of affected rows,\n    like a `delete` command without returning would)","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"* `:log` - When false, does not log the query\n  * `:timeout` - Execute request timeout, accepts: `:infinity` (default: `15000`);","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3-options"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> MyRepo.query(\"SELECT $1::integer + $2\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, %{rows: [[42]], num_rows: 1}}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(MyRepo, \"SELECT $1::integer + $2\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, %{rows: [[42]], num_rows: 1}}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Same as `query/3` but returns result directly without `:ok` tuple\nand raises on invalid queries","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query!/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query!/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Runs a custom SQL query that returns multiple results on the given repo.\n\nIn case of success, it must return an `:ok` tuple containing a list of\nmaps with at least two keys:\n\n  * `:num_rows` - the number of rows affected\n\n  * `:rows` - the result set as a list. `nil` may be returned\n    instead of the list if the command does not yield any row\n    as result (but still yields the number of affected rows,\n    like a `delete` command without returning would)","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"* `:log` - When false, does not log the query\n  * `:timeout` - Execute request timeout, accepts: `:infinity` (default: `15000`);","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3-options"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> MyRepo.query_many(\"SELECT $1; SELECT $2;\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, [%{rows: [[40]], num_rows: 1}, %{rows: [[2]], num_rows: 1}]}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query_many(MyRepo, \"SELECT $1; SELECT $2;\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, [%{rows: [[40]], num_rows: 1}, %{rows: [[2]], num_rows: 1}]}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Same as `query_many/4` but returns result directly without `:ok` tuple\nand raises on invalid queries","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query_many!/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many!/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.reload/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.reload/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#reload/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.reload!/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.reload!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#reload!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.rollback/1 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.rollback/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#rollback/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.start_link/1 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.start_link/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.stop/1 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.stop/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#stop/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.stream/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.stream/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#stream/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Converts the given query to SQL according to its kind and the\nadapter in the given repository.","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.to_sql/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#to_sql/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"The examples below are meant for reference. Each adapter will\nreturn a different result:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.to_sql(:all, Post)\n    {\"SELECT p.id, p.title, p.inserted_at, p.created_at FROM posts as p\", []}\n\n    iex> MyRepo.to_sql(:update_all, from(p in Post, update: [set: [title: ^\"hello\"]]))\n    {\"UPDATE posts AS p SET title = $1\", [\"hello\"]}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.to_sql(:all, MyRepo, Post)\n    {\"SELECT p.id, p.title, p.inserted_at, p.created_at FROM posts as p\", []}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.to_sql/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#to_sql/3-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.transact/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.transact/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#transact/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.transaction/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.transaction/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#transaction/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update!/2 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update!/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update_all/3 .","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update_all/3","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update_all/3"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for publication sources: free-text provenance entries that record how\na publication's data was collected, so a reader can verify the claim. Owned by a\nsingle publication (deleted with it), ordered, and edited inline via the\npublication update path — never shared or deduplicated across publications.","title":"RichardBurton.Source","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Reverse of `nest/1`: an ordered list of the source strings. Accepts loaded\nchild structs (ordered by `position`) or an already-flat list; anything else\n(nil, `%NotLoaded{}`) flattens to an empty list.","title":"RichardBurton.Source.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Source.flatten([\n  ...>   %RichardBurton.Source{content: \"Second\", position: 1},\n  ...>   %RichardBurton.Source{content: \"First\", position: 0}\n  ...> ])\n  [\"First\", \"Second\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.flatten(nil)\n  []","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Source.flatten/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","doc":"Turn the flat wire shape (an ordered list of free-text strings) into the nested\nchild maps `cast_assoc(:sources)` expects, assigning `position` from order.\nBlank rows are dropped so an empty editor row doesn't fail validation.","title":"RichardBurton.Source.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest([\"First source\", \"Second source\"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"First source\", \"position\" => 0}, %{\"content\" => \"Second source\", \"position\" => 1}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest([\"A source\", \"  \"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"A source\", \"position\" => 0}]\n\n  A blank in the middle is dropped and positions stay contiguous — the entry\n  after it is `1`, not `2`:\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest([\"First\", \"\", \"Second\"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"First\", \"position\" => 0}, %{\"content\" => \"Second\", \"position\" => 1}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest(nil)\n  []","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Source.nest/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"module","doc":"A translated book: an original book, and the people who translated it.\n\nTwo translated books of the same original book by the same translators are\nthe same book, and the database keeps only one (see\n`RichardBurton.Identity`). `authors_fingerprint` is the fingerprint of the\ntranslators' names, which the database writes, so the schema never writes it.","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.preload/1","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#preload/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Schema for users","title":"RichardBurton.User","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.User.all/0","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Whether `role` is `required`, or outranks it.","title":"RichardBurton.User.at_least?/2","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#at_least?/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"The role named by `role`, given as a string or an atom.\n\nNames arrive as strings from request params, the console and the dev\nprovider; only this knows which strings are roles.","title":"RichardBurton.User.cast_role/1","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#cast_role/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Deletes a user's account and all of their sessions, on behalf of `actor`.\n`actor` is the subject id of the person asking, or `nil` from the console.\nReturns `{:ok, user}`. Like `set_role/3`, it refuses to remove your own\naccount or the last admin's, and returns `{:error, :self}` or\n`{:error, :last_admin}`.\n\nDeleting the sessions signs the person out everywhere. The sessions and the\naccount are deleted in one transaction. After the transaction commits,\n`delete/2` calls `RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1`, so the person's open\ndocument channels check their access again and close.\n`Session.revoke_all/1` also calls `changed/1`, but that call happens inside\nthe transaction, too early to close them.","title":"RichardBurton.User.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.User.get/1","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.User.get_by_email/1","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get_by_email/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.User.get_by_id/1","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get_by_id/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.User.insert/1","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#insert/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"The roles, least privileged first. A role admits everything the ones before it\nadmit: a contributor adds and corrects publications, an admin also decides who may.","title":"RichardBurton.User.roles/0","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#roles/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Give a user a role, on behalf of `actor` — the subject id of whoever is\nasking, or `nil` from the console.\n\nTwo changes are refused: your own role, and the one that would leave the\ndatabase without an admin. Answers `{:error, :self}` or `{:error, :last_admin}`\nrespectively, and `{:error, :invalid_role}` for a role that is not one.","title":"RichardBurton.User.set_role/3","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#set_role/3"},{"type":"module","doc":"Utilities for general purpose","title":"RichardBurton.Util","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Util.deep_merge_maps/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#deep_merge_maps/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Given an enumerable with atom keys, return the enumerable with string keys instead.\n  Works for keyword lists, key-value tuples, maps and structs.\n  When an ordinary list is given, it stringify the keys of its elements.\n  Does not traverse structures other than ordinary lists.","title":"RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#stringify_keys/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{})\n      %{}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{\"key\" => \"value\"})\n      %{\"key\" => \"value\"}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({\"key\", \"value\"})\n      {\"key\", \"value\"}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([])\n      []\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({:key, \"value\"})\n      {\"key\", \"value\"}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({:key, :value})\n      {\"key\", :value}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([key1: \"value\", key2: 1234])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([key: :value])\n      [{\"key\", :value}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, {:key2, 1234}])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key1: \"value\", key2: 1234})\n      %{\"key1\" => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key: :value})\n      %{\"key\" => :value}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{:key1 => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234})\n      %{\"key1\" => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, %{key2: 1234}])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, %{\"key2\" => 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key: {:key, \"value\"}})\n      %{\"key\" => {:key, \"value\"}}","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#stringify_keys/1-examples"},{"type":"module","doc":"Utilities for data validation","title":"RichardBurton.Validation","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.get_errors/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#get_errors/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Rejects repeated entries in a cast association, comparing each child by\n`key`. Without this a publication carrying the same country, publisher or\nauthor twice reaches the join tables' unique indexes and raises there,\nturning a data-entry slip into a 500.\n\nErrors are reported under `field` unless `:as` names another key. Nested\nerrors collapse to their innermost map, so an original book's `authors`\nwould otherwise arrive indistinguishable from the translators' — `:as` puts\nit under the flat name the client knows it by.","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.validate_no_duplicates/4","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#validate_no_duplicates/4"},{"type":"module","doc":"Lists, checks and renames the names that publications are built from:\nauthors, who are translators or original authors, and publishers.\n\nThese names are typed by hand, so one name can be stored under two\nspellings, such as \"Penguin books\" and \"Penguin Books\", or \"Alfred A.Knopf\"\nand \"Alfred A. Knopf\". Each spelling is a separate record, and each\npublication links to only one of them.\n\nA *kind* is `\"authors\"` or `\"publishers\"`, the word the routes use for each\nschema.","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"`rename/4` is the only function that changes a name. There is no separate\nmerge. Names are unique within a kind, so a rename has one of two outcomes:\n\n  * `:renamed` means the new name is free, and the record takes it.\n  * `:merged` means another record, the *keeper*, already has the new name.\n    The renamed record is *folded* into the keeper: its links move to the\n    keeper and the record is deleted.\n\nA fold cannot be undone, so `rename/4` folds only when its `folding?`\nargument is true.","title":"Renaming and folding - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-renaming-and-folding"},{"type":"module","doc":"The composite keys of books and publications include fingerprints of these\nnames, which the database recomputes when a name changes or a link moves\n(see `RichardBurton.Identity`). A publisher's name is part of a\npublication's key. An author's name is part of the key of each original\nbook they wrote and each translated book they translated.\n\nWhen a rename gives a book the same key as an older book, `rename/4` folds\nthe newer book into the older one, in the same transaction: the newer book's\ntranslations, or its publications, move to the older book, and the newer\nbook is deleted. When two original books are folded and each has a\ntranslation by the same translators, those two translations are folded into\none as well.","title":"Books that become one - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-books-that-become-one"},{"type":"module","doc":"A rename can give two publications the same composite key: the same title,\nyear and translated book, and the same publishers and countries. This\nhappens when one publication was entered twice and the two copies differ\nonly in the spelling of a name. The composite key applies to the\npublications that are not deleted, so the database cannot store both.\n\nIn that case `rename/4` rolls back and returns\n`{:error, {:would_collide, publications}}`, listing the two publications. It\ndoes not merge them. Merging publications is done by\n`RichardBurton.Publication.merge/3`, which takes the publication to keep and\ncan be undone.\n\nDeleted publications follow the rename as well, so a restored publication\nmatches its names. They are never reported as a collision, because the\ncomposite key does not cover them.","title":"Renames that would duplicate a publication - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-renames-that-would-duplicate-a-publication"},{"type":"module","doc":"`resemblances/2` compares names that are not stored yet with the stored\nnames. A name is *held* when a record has exactly that name. A name\n*resembles* a stored name when the two differ but their trigram similarity,\nwith accents removed, is above 0.7. A name that is neither held nor\nresembles a stored name is new.\n\nTwo publishers' names also resemble each other when one contains the other's\nwords, as \"Alfred A. Knopf\" contains \"Knopf\". Authors' names do not, because\none author record can hold two people, such as \"William L. Grossman & Helen\nCaldwell\", and each person's own name would then resemble it.","title":"Checking names before they are entered - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-checking-names-before-they-are-entered"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns every name of the given kind, sorted by name, as\n`%{id:, name:, publications:, resembles:}`.\n\n`publications` counts the publications that credit the name and are not\ndeleted. `resembles` lists the ids of the other names of this kind that the\nname resembles. Each of those ids is also in the returned list.\n\nReturns `{:error, :no_such_kind}` when the kind is unknown.","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.all/1","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#all/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the kinds of name, as the words the routes use.","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.kinds/0","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#kinds/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Renames the name with the given id. When another name of the same kind\nalready has the new name, folds this one into it.\n\nThe new name is trimmed. Returns `{:ok, :renamed}` when the record takes the\nnew name, and `{:ok, :merged}` when it is folded. After either, it calls\n`Refresher.refresh/0`.\n\nA fold happens only when `folding?` is true. When it is false and the name is\ntaken, nothing is written and the result is\n`{:error, {:would_fold, keeper, publications}}`. `keeper` is\n`%{id:, name:, publications:}`. `publications` lists the publications that\ncredit the renamed record and are not deleted, as `%{id:, title:, year:}`,\nsorted by title and year. A rename onto a free name needs no flag, because\nrenaming back undoes it.\n\nThe other errors are `{:error, {:would_collide, publications}}` (see the\nmodule doc), `{:error, :blank}`, `{:error, :not_found}` and\n`{:error, :no_such_kind}`.","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.rename/4","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#rename/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Compares the given names with the stored names of the given kind.\n\nReturns `{:ok, entries}`, with one `%{name:, held:, resembles:}` per name.\n`held` is true when a stored name matches exactly. `resembles` lists the\nstored names that resemble it, as `%{id:, name:, publications:}`, with the\nmost used first.\n\nThe names are trimmed, blank names are dropped, and a repeated name appears\nonce. Returns `{:error, :no_such_kind}` when the kind is unknown.","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.resemblances/2","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#resemblances/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The entrypoint for defining your web interface, such\nas controllers, views, channels and so on.\n\nThis can be used in your application as:\n\n    use RichardBurtonWeb, :controller\n    use RichardBurtonWeb, :view\n\nThe definitions below will be executed for every view,\ncontroller, etc, so keep them short and clean, focused\non imports, uses and aliases.\n\nDo NOT define functions inside the quoted expressions\nbelow. Instead, define any helper function in modules\nand import those modules here.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html"},{"type":"macro","doc":"When used, dispatch to the appropriate controller/view/etc.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.__using__/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#__using__/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.channel/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#channel/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.controller/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#controller/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.router/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#router/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.view/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#view/0"},{"type":"module","doc":"The author lookup that backs the editor's autocomplete: every author, or\nthose matching a search term.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The country lookup that backs the editor's country field: every country, or\nthose a term finds, named in the language asked for.\n\nA country is stored as its code and shown as its name, so both travel: the id\nis what a publication holds, the label is what a reader is shown.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Dev/test-only credentials provider. Mints an `rb-session` (plus the readable\n`csrf-token`) without the Google OAuth handshake, so the admin UI can be\nexercised locally.\n\nA role can be asked for — `%{\"role\" => \"contributor\"}` — so the surfaces an\nadmin sees and a contributor does not can be exercised as each.\n\nKept out of production two ways: this file lives outside `lib/`, on a compile\npath only added for `:dev`, `:test` and `:e2e` (see `elixirc_paths` in\n`mix.exs`), so the\nmodule is not compiled into a prod release at all; and the route is wired only\nwhen `Mix.env() in [:dev, :test, :e2e]` (see the router).","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Upserts the dev user for the asked-for role and sets its session cookies.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.create/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.html#create/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Relays one import document's changes between the people who have it open,\nand tracks who they are.\n\nThe list of documents is shared, so anyone who may edit publications may join\nany document. Joining a document that does not exist is refused with\n`not_found`.\n\nA change is an opaque Yjs update, the same bytes the document endpoints\nstore. The channel only relays changes and does not parse or store them. A\nchange that a connection misses is still in the stored updates.\n\nThe channel carries three other kinds of message:\n\n  * **presence** — who has the document open. Each connection is tracked\n    under the person the socket authenticated, with the email address stored\n    for that person, so a connection cannot claim to be someone else. Phoenix\n    untracks a connection when its process ends, including when it crashes,\n    and broadcasts the leave to the other connections.\n  * **awareness** — where each person's cursor is. The channel relays the\n    opaque Yjs awareness bytes and does not store them, because they only\n    matter while the person is connected. A connection can send anything\n    about its own cursor, but the identity paired with the cursor comes from\n    presence.\n  * **sync** — a Yjs state vector, which summarises the changes a connection\n    has. It carries the connection it is `from` and, in a reply, the one it\n    is `to`. A connection sends one when it joins, and the other connections\n    reply with the changes it lacks. This covers changes that were relayed\n    before it joined but were not yet stored.\n\n`topic/1` names a document's topic. `relay!/2` sends an update to the\nconnections on a document from outside the channel, as the same `update`\nmessage a connection relays.\n\nAfter a connection joins, the channel calls\n`RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.allowed?/2` again to check that the person\nsigned in on the connection still has access. It checks each time\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Access` broadcasts a change of access for that person,\nand every five minutes, because a session that expires is not broadcast. If\nthe check fails, the channel pushes `refused` and closes.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.child_spec/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Broadcasts an `update`, `awareness` or `sync` message to the other\nconnections on the document, and ignores any other event.\n\n`broadcast_from!` skips the sender, so the sender does not receive its own\nchange and apply it a second time.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.handle_in/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#handle_in/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Sends `update`, a base64 Yjs update, to the connections on the document with\nthe id `id`, as an `update` message.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.relay!/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#relay!/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.start_link/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the topic of the document with the id `id`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.topic/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#topic/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"The import-document endpoints: creating, listing, renaming, archiving and\nunarchiving documents, and reading, appending and compacting their updates.\n\nThe list is shared, so no endpoint scopes it to the person asking. Updates\nare sent and returned as base64 strings in JSON, like the rest of this API.\nThe server does not parse them. See `RichardBurton.Document`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Appends one base64 update to a document, with an optional `rows` count. A\nmissing or invalid `rows` leaves the stored count unchanged.\n\nWith `relay` set to true, the update is also broadcast as an `update` message\nto the connections on the document's channel, as if a connection had sent it.\nA connection relays its own edits, so `relay` is for an update from a writer\nthat is not connected to the document.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.append/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#append/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Archives a document, which takes it off the list and keeps its content.\n\nThis action serves `DELETE /documents/:id` but does not delete anything,\nbecause the rows are a record of what was prepared.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.archive/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#archive/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Replaces every update up to `through` with one merged update.\n\nThe caller builds the merged update, because the server does not parse the\ncontent. `through` is the id returned by the read the merge was built from,\nand updates with a larger id are kept. See `RichardBurton.Document.compact/3`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.compact/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#compact/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.create/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a page of documents and whether more follow it, as\n`RichardBurton.Document.page/1` does. With `archived=true` the page holds\narchived documents instead of unarchived ones. `limit` sets the page size.\n\n`after` is the cursor: the `updated_at` and `id` of the last document on the\nprevious page. Without it, the first page is returned. A malformed cursor is\na 400 with `invalid_after`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.show/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#show/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Unarchives a document, which puts it back on the list.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.unarchive/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#unarchive/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Renames a document. A request without `name` is a 400 with `name_required`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.update/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the document's updates as base64 `entries`, and `through`, the id of\nthe last update returned.\n\nApplying the entries rebuilds the document's content. A compaction built\nfrom these entries sends `through` back, so that it keeps any update\nappended after this read.\n\nWith `after`, only the updates written after that id are returned.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.updates/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#updates/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The socket for editing import documents live, with one\n`RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel` per document.\n\nIt authenticates with the `rb-session` cookie, as HTTP requests do. The\nconnect request carries the cookie, and `RichardBurtonWeb.SessionCookie`\npasses its value to `connect/3`. Connecting counts as use of the session:\n`Session.verify/1` slides its idle timeout. The later checks use\n`Session.active?/1`, which does not.\n\nA connect request must also carry the CSRF token from the `csrf-token`\ncookie, in the auth-token header rather than the URL, and the token must be\nfor the session's person. This is the double-submit check that\n`RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf` makes for HTTP requests. A connect\nrequest sent from another origin can carry the session cookie, but its\nsender cannot read the CSRF token, so the request is refused.\n\n`allowed?/2` is checked on every connect and every join, in the same way\n`Plugs.Authorize` checks every request.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns whether the session is still active and the person still has the\ncontributor role or a higher one.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.allowed?/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.html#allowed?/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Resets the database between Playwright tests: truncates every table, restarts\nidentity sequences, and refreshes the search index. Mounted only in the :e2e\nenvironment (see the router). Each worker owns its own database, so a reset\nonly clears that worker's data — this is what keeps parallel workers isolated.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.create/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.html#create/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The public contact form. The only unauthenticated write, so it is guarded by\nrecaptcha rather than by a session.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.contact/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.html#contact/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The HTTP entry point: the socket, the static and parsing plugs, and the\nsession the `rb-session` cookie is carried in.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast/3 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast!/3 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast!/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast!/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast_from/4 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast_from/4","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast_from/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast_from!/4 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast_from!/4","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast_from!/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Plug.call/2 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the child specification to start the endpoint\nunder a supervision tree.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.child_spec/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the endpoint configuration for `key`\n\nReturns `default` if the key does not exist.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.config/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#config/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Reloads the configuration given the application environment changes.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.config_change/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#config_change/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the host for the given endpoint.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.host/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#host/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Plug.init/1 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.local_broadcast/3 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.local_broadcast/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#local_broadcast/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.local_broadcast_from/4 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.local_broadcast_from/4","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#local_broadcast_from/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates the path information when routing to this endpoint.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.path/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#path/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates the script name.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.script_name/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#script_name/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the address and port that the server is running on","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.server_info/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#server_info/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Starts the endpoint supervision tree.\n\nAll other options are merged into the endpoint configuration.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.start_link/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates a base64-encoded cryptographic hash (sha512) to a static file\nin `priv/static`. Meant to be used for Subresource Integrity with CDNs.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_integrity/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_integrity/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns a two item tuple with the first item being the `static_path`\nand the second item being the `static_integrity`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_lookup/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_lookup/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates a route to a static file in `priv/static`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_path/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_path/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates the static URL without any path information.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:static_url` to generate\nsuch. It falls back to `:url` if `:static_url` is not set.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_url/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_url/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates the endpoint base URL but as a `URI` struct.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:url` to generate such.\nUseful for manipulating the URL data and passing it to\nURL helpers.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.struct_url/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#struct_url/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.subscribe/2 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.subscribe/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#subscribe/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.unsubscribe/1 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.unsubscribe/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#unsubscribe/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Generates the endpoint base URL without any path information.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:url` to generate such.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.url/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#url/0"},{"type":"module","doc":"Conveniences for translating and building error messages.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorHelpers","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorHelpers.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Renders errors as the status text alone, since this is a headless API and the\nclient renders its own pages.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"The resource name, as an atom, for this view","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.__resource__/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html#__resource__/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Renders the given template locally.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.render/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html#render/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback invoked when no template is found.\nBy default it raises but can be customized\nto render a particular template.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.template_not_found/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html#template_not_found/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Turns an error returned by a controller action into a JSON response.\n\nEvery controller sets this as its `action_fallback` (see `RichardBurtonWeb`),\nso an action's `with` can return the first error it meets. Each kind of error\nthen gets the same status and body from every endpoint.\n\n  * `{:error, :not_found}` is a 404.\n  * `{:error, reason}` with an atom is a 409 when the reason is a conflict,\n    and a 400 otherwise. The body is `%{error: reason}`.\n  * `{:error, status, reason}` responds with that status and\n    `%{error: reason}`. An action returns it when it needs a different status\n    from the one the reason normally gets.\n  * `{:error, changeset}`, or `{:error, errors}` with a map of errors by field,\n    is a 400 whose body is `%{errors: errors}`.\n\nA **conflict** is a valid request that is refused because of the current\nstate of the data, as opposed to a malformed request. The conflicts are a\nrecord that already exists (`:conflict`), a record held inside another\n(`:absorbed`), demoting the last admin (`:last_admin`), changing one's own\nrole (`:self`), and an invitation already accepted (`:accepted`) or already\npending (`:pending`).","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns `{:ok, record}`, or `{:error, :not_found}` when `record` is `nil`.\nIt lets a lookup that returns `nil` be used in a `with`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.found/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.html#found/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Returns the counts from `RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.describe/1` for\nevery publication in the index, or for the ones the `search` parameter\nmatches. The search is parsed the same way as an index search.\n\nThe response also has a `matched` key. With a search, it holds\n`Publication.Index.Excerpt.resolution/1` for the search: the words the search\nmatched with something other than what was typed. Without a search, it is\n`nil`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController.show/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController.html#show/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Invitations: offering a role to an address, listing what is outstanding,\nresending, and withdrawing.\n\nAn invitation is redeemed by signing in with the address it names, so nothing\nhere creates an account.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Offer a role to an address.\n\nAnswers with what happened, because the three outcomes are genuinely\ndifferent: someone already here was granted the role, someone new has an\ninvitation waiting, or they have one waiting that could not be mailed to them.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.create/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Withdraw a pending invitation.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Every invitation — the ones waiting, and the ones taken up.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Send a pending invitation's mail again.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.resend/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#resend/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The original-book lookup that backs the editor's autocomplete, returning a book\nwhole — title and authors together — so selecting one fills both.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Authenticates a request via an identity-provider token in the\n`Authorization: Bearer` header (see `RichardBurton.Auth`) and assigns\n`:subject_id` and `:email`. Used by the login endpoints that exchange the\nprovider token for a session.\n\nThe email is assigned from the verified token, not read from the request, so\nwhat the account is keyed on is the provider's word rather than the caller's.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Authenticates a request via the app's own `rb-session` cookie\n(see `RichardBurton.Auth.Session`) and assigns `:subject_id`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Authorizes the request when the authenticated subject (assigned by an\n`Authenticate` plug) holds the given role, or one that outranks it.\n\n    plug RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize, role: :contributor\n\nA route names the least role it needs, so a role ranked above it is admitted\nwithout revisiting every route. The role is read per request, so granting or\nrevoking takes effect on the next one rather than at the next sign-in.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Plug for recaptcha verification","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Removes a `search` parameter holding nothing but whitespace, so that the\nrequest is answered as though it carried no search at all.\n\nAn empty term matches nothing and an absent one asks for everything, so the two\nanswers are opposites rather than neighbours. A caller that builds its query\nstring from an empty input would get an empty database back, which is why every\nsuch caller otherwise has to remember to leave the parameter out. This removes\nit once, for every action that reads one.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Double-submit CSRF check for state-changing admin requests. Safe methods\n(GET/HEAD/OPTIONS) pass through untouched. For unsafe methods it requires an\n`rb-csrf-token` header carrying a valid `RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf` token whose\nsubject matches the authenticated session (assigned by `Authenticate.Cookie`,\nwhich must run first). Defense in depth on top of the `SameSite=Lax` cookie.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","doc":"Tracks who has each import document open.\n\n`RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel` tracks each joined connection under the\n`subject_id` the socket authenticated, with the person's email address and\nthe connection's `client_id`. When a connection closes, or its process dies\nwithout closing cleanly, Phoenix untracks it and broadcasts the leave to the\nother connections on the document.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.child_spec/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.fetch/2 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.fetch/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#fetch/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.fetchers_pids/0","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#fetchers_pids/0"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.get_by_key/2 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.get_by_key/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#get_by_key/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.list/1 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.list/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#list/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.track/3 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.track/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#track/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.track/4 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.track/4","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#track/4"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.untrack/2 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.untrack/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#untrack/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.untrack/3 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.untrack/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#untrack/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.update/3 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.update/3","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#update/3"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.update/4 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.update/4","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#update/4"},{"type":"module","doc":"The publication endpoints: reading the index, searching and exporting it, and\nthe admin writes — insert, update, merge, delete, restore and undo.\n\nReads are paged by a frozen ordering rather than by offset. The first response\nto a query carries the whole ordering — every matching id, in reading order —\nplus the first page of rows; later pages are fetched by id against that\nordering, so results cannot drift as the database changes underneath a reader.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.create_all/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#create_all/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.distinctions/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#distinctions/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.distinguish/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#distinguish/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.duplicates/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#duplicates/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.export/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#export/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.history/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#history/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.index_deleted/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#index_deleted/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.merge/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#merge/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.reconsider/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#reconsider/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.resemblances/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#resemblances/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.restore/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#restore/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.show/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#show/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.undo/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#undo/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.update/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.validate/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#validate/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The publisher lookup that backs the editor's autocomplete: every publisher, or\nthose matching a search term.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"The routes, grouped by what each pipeline requires: public reads, writes\nbehind a session and a role, and the development-only helpers that are never\nmounted in production.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.api/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#api/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authenticate_bearer/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authenticate_bearer/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_admin/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_admin/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_contributor/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_contributor/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_recaptcha/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_recaptcha/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback invoked by Plug on every request.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.call/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.files/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#files/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.VerifiedRoutes.formatted_routes/1 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.formatted_routes/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#formatted_routes/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback required by Plug that initializes the router\nfor serving web requests.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.init/1","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.VerifiedRoutes.verified_route?/2 .","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.verified_route?/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#verified_route?/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Exchanges a verified identity-provider token for the app's own session cookie.\n\nThe `Authenticate` plug has already verified the incoming provider token and\nassigned the identity it vouches for; this action upserts the user and sets\nthe `rb-session` cookie so subsequent requests authenticate without forwarding\nthe provider token.\n\nThe account is keyed on the assigned subject and email — never on the request\nbody, which the caller controls and which an invitation would otherwise let\nthem turn into a role.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.create/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Clears the `rb-session` cookie so the browser is signed out. The cookie is\nhttp-only, so only a server response can expire it; no auth is required since\nclearing one's own cookie is harmless.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"A read-only `Plug.Session.Store` that returns the raw value of the\n`rb-session` cookie as `%{\"token\" => token}`.\n\nPhoenix passes a cookie to a socket's `connect/3` only as a session read\nthrough a `Plug.Session` store. The `rb-session` cookie holds a session token,\nnot a Plug session, so this store returns the token as it is, without\ndecoding or checking it.\n\nThe store cannot write. `put/4` and `delete/3` raise, because sessions are\ncreated and revoked by `RichardBurton.Auth.Session`.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionCookie","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionCookie.html"},{"type":"module","doc":"Who may work on the database: the signed-in subject, the list of accounts, and\nthe admin writes that change or revoke a role.\n\nThe platform refuses to be left without an administrator, and nobody may\nchange their own role.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"Revoke someone's access, and the sessions signed in as them.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.delete/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Everyone with access, and what they may do.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns the current user for a valid `rb-session` cookie, or `null`. Public\n(reads the cookie itself) so the SPA can poll auth state without a 401.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.me/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#me/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Change what a user may do.\n\nThe last admin cannot be demoted: the platform would be left with nobody who\ncan grant access, and no way back except the database.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.update/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#update/2"},{"type":"module","doc":"Lists, checks and renames the author and publisher names that publications\nare built from. See `RichardBurton.Vocabulary`.\n\nThere are no create or delete actions. A name is created when a publication\nthat uses it is saved. It is deleted only when a rename folds it into another\nname, and a name that no publication uses stays in the list.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html"},{"type":"function","doc":"","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.index/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Returns, for each name in `\"names\"`, whether it is already stored and which\nstored names it resembles. See `RichardBurton.Vocabulary.resemblances/2`.\n\nThis action writes nothing. It is a POST because the list of names can be\ntoo long for a URL.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.resemblances/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html#resemblances/2"},{"type":"function","doc":"Renames a name, and responds with `outcome` set to `\"renamed\"` or\n`\"merged\"`. See `RichardBurton.Vocabulary.rename/4`.\n\nFolding into a name that is already taken requires `\"fold\" => true`. Without\nit, nothing is written and the response is 409 with `error: \"would_fold\"`,\n`into`, the name that has it, and `publications`, the publications that\ncredit the name being renamed. A rename that would give two publications the\nsame identity is also a 409, with `error: \"would_collide\"` and the\n`publications` involved.","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.update/2","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html#update/2"}],"proglang":"elixir","content_type":"text/markdown","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.4"}}